player discarded J1, previous discarded J3 \u2014 so J2 might be live. But I only have one \u2014 not urgent to keep unless aiming for dragon pung.\n- Play F1: Already discarded by others (next player played F1, across played F1 twice), so likely safe. But still, not useful to me.\n- Play B5: Middle dot, single. Not helpful. Could be part of chow, but weak.\n\nBest discards: remove terminals and isolated honors.\n\nTop candidates: T1, B1, B5, F1, F4, J2\n\nAmong these, **T1 and B1** are terminals and least connected.\n\nBut **B1** pairs with B2 \u2014 B1+B2 could be start of chow B1-B2-B3? Yes! That\u2019s possible. I have B1 and B2\u00d73 \u2014 so if I draw B3, I can form B1-B2-B3 chow. So **B1 + B2\u00d73** is actually a strong foundation for **chows in Dots**!\n\nThat changes things.\n\nWith B1, B2\u00d73, I can:\n- Form chow [B1,B2,B3] if I draw B3\n- Or use B2\u00d73 as pung\n- Or even aim for **Lower Tiles** if I collect B3, W1-W3, T1-T3, etc.\n\nBut I have W7-W9 \u2014 conflicting direction.\n\nAlternatively, I could commit to **Dots chows + pung**.\n\nBut W7-W8-W9 \u2014 if I get more W6/W7/W8/W9, could form multiple chows.\n\nBut I only have one W8, one W9 \u2014 two W7. So best wait is **closed wait on W8** for [W7,W8,W9]? No \u2014 I need one W8, but I already have one. To make a chow [W7,W8,W9], I need one of each. I have two W7, one W8, one W9 \u2014 so I can form one such chow, leaving an extra W7.\n\nSo possible to form [W7,W8,W9] + [W7,?,?] \u2014 but second W7 needs W6+W7 or W7+W8 again.\n\nSo [W6,W7,W8] is possible if I draw W6.\n\nSo W6, W7, W8, W9 all useful.\n\nThus, **W7 is valuable** \u2014 I should keep at least one, preferably both until I complete a meld.\n\nSimilarly, **T1 and T9** \u2014 isolated. T1 could be used in T1-T2-T3, but T2/T3? Unshown: T3:1 (low), T2:4 \u2014 T3 is scarce. Previous player had T3-T4-T5 \u2014 so T3 is out, probably gone. So T1-T2-T3 unlikely.\n\nT9: T7/T8 needed \u2014 unknown.\n\nSo T1 and T9 are weak.\n\nB1 + B2\u00d73 is strong \u2014 keep.\n\nF1, F4, J2: honors, no pungs yet. J2 has full supply, but no support.\n\nSo best to discard **T1 or T9 or an honor**.\n\nNow look at safety:  \nNext player has melded [W1,W2,W3] and [J3,J3,J3] \u2014 so likely going for pure Character chows or winds/dragons. They discarded W5, W9 \u2014 so W9 may be safe? But they discarded W9 after having W1-W2-W3 \u2014 possibly not collecting W9s.\n\nBut W9 is terminal \u2014 often safer.\n\nHowever, **F4**: I have one, unshown F4:2 \u2014 2 left, so not dead. Across player discarded F4 twice \u2014 so may not want it. Next player didn\u2019t pick up F4 \u2014 so maybe safe.\n\nBut among choices, **T1** seems weakest:  \n- Terminal  \n- Bamboo 1 \u2014 but I have B1+B2\u00d73 \u2014 so B1 supports chow, T1 does not connect to anything  \n- No T2 or T3 in hand  \n- T3 is rare (only 1 left), T2:4 \u2014 but previous player has T3-T4-T5 \u2014 so T2 might help them for T2-T3-T4? But they have T3,T4,T5 \u2014 so T2 could extend? Unlikely \u2014 they\u2019d need T1-T2-T3 or T2-T3-T4. But they discarded T3? No \u2014 they claimed T4 via Chi, then discarded T5, etc. Their meld is [T3,T4,T5] \u2014 so they have one T3. There were 4 total \u2014 unshown: T3:1 \u2014 so one still in wall or hand. So T2 might be useful to someone.\n\nBut I have no connection.\n\nSo **T1** is the most disconnected tile.\n\nSimilarly, **T9** \u2014 no T7/T8 \u2014 less useful.\n\nBut T9 is higher point? No.\n\nAnother angle: **Self-preservation** \u2014 avoid feeding opponents.\n\nNext player has [W1,W2,W3] \u2014 so W4 might help them for continuity? But not directly. W4 is safe.\n\nBut I don\u2019t have W4.\n\nI have W7,W8,W9 \u2014 W8 and W9 could help someone with W6-W7-W8 or W7-W8-W9.\n\nBut W7 I have two \u2014 discarding one W7 is okay, but still risky.\n\nBest discard: **T1** \u2014 completely isolated, no use in my hand, and not part of any future chow given lack of T2/T3.\n\nAlternatively, **J2** \u2014 but J2 is dragon, and I have only one \u2014 but could develop into pung. And dragons score points (Pung = 2 points, Two Dragon Pungs = 6, etc.). But currently, B2 pung is better.\n\nBut I can keep J2 for now.\n\nF1: similar \u2014 but F1 is wind, not prevalent (West=F3) or seat (North=F4). So **no scoring chance** for F1 pung. So worse than J2.\n\nJ2 is dragon \u2014 always scores if pung formed.\n\nSo better to keep J2 than F1.\n\nF4: my seat wind! Seat wind is North Wind = F4.\n\nI have F4 \u2014 if I can form a pung of F4, I get **Seat Wind** pattern (2 points).\n\nUnshown F4:2 \u2014 two left. Possible.\n\nAcross player discarded F4 twice \u2014 so maybe they don\u2019t want it. But could be bluff.\n\nStill, possible to collect.\n\nSo F4 has potential.\n\nF1: no such benefit.\n\nSo F1 is worse than F4.\n\nThus, worst tiles: T1, B1, B5, F1\n\nB1 connects to B2 \u2014 so keep.\n\nB5: no connection \u2014 could be part of B4-B5-B6 or B5-B6-B7, but B6-B7-B8 is exposed by previous player \u2014 so B6,B7,B8 are gone \u2014 so B5 cannot form chow with them. B4: unshown:3 \u2014 possible, but B3:2 \u2014 possible. Still, B4-B5-B6 is possible.\n\nBut I have no B4/B6 \u2014 so B5 is weak.\n\nBut compared to T1 \u2014 both weak.\n\nBut T1 has no wind/honor value, no connection \u2014 so **T1 is the worst tile**.\n\nTherefore, best play: **Play T1**\n\nAnswer: Play T1\n\nFinal decision:\n\nEvaluation: My hand contains a strong concealed pung of B2 and a partial chow in W7-W9. I also hold several terminal and honor tiles that are not contributing to a clear pattern. Among the discards, T1 is completely isolated, with no connections to other tiles in hand and limited usefulness to opponents. Other tiles like B1 support potential chows with B2, while F4 offers a chance for Seat Wind. T1 provides no such value.\nReason: Discarding T1 removes a useless terminal tile without disrupting potential melds or aiding opponents significantly.\nAnswer: Play T1"}
